    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d828">

                <pb facs="0910" n="1744" sameAs="frus:frus1918Supp01v02/pg1744"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 652.119/1748</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The War Trade Board Representative in Cuba</hi>
                        (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Morgan</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Habana</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1918-09-18T17:00:00-05:29">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">September 18, 1918, 5 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received 9.24 p.m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Your August 21, 4 p.m., and my August 22. It is reported here that agreement has
                    been reached in Washington with Spanish lines to coal all their boats at Porto
                    Rico or other American ports to take effect on October 1. Please advise me if
                    this is correct.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d828-f1"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Morgan</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d828-f1" n="1" xml:id="d828-f1-content">
                <p>Department’s memorandum attached to this telegram reads: “Sept. 20, 1918. Mr.
                        Fuller [of the War
                    Trade Board] telephoned to say that he was informed by Mr.
                        Richards [of the Board] that no such agreement had been
                    made but that the agent of the Pinillos Line had assured him that they were
                    quite prepared to have their ships stop at Porto Rico for censorship purposes
                    and that the line would undertake to do this without having the matter taken up
                    diplomatically. L[eland] H[arrison.]” The telegram sent in
                    reply, Sept. 20, merely stated “no such agreement has been made.”</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
